## How they are written (abstracts and the start of the introductions of three read, the structure of all measured by script)

- **Abstract.** One paragraph, no section label beyond "Abstract". Order: the cost or difficulty that motivates the work (evaluation is expensive / labels are scarce / benchmarks are unreliable) → what the paper does, in a sentence or two → the headline finding stated as a finding, often with a size (an error reduction, "N benchmarks, M models") → what it is good for or its scope. Several state the amount of data used (e.g. "30 benchmarks and 375 open-weight models").
- **Introduction.** About five to seven paragraphs: why the problem matters now → what existing practice does and where it falls short, with citations → what this paper does ("In this work we …") → the result in one or two sentences. Contributions are mostly stated in prose: the word "contribution" appears in the introduction of 9 of 14, and bulleted lists were found in only two (1 and 3 items). A first figure that carries the idea or the headline result is on page 1 or 2 in at least 9 of 14 (the position could not be read reliably for the others).
- **Sections.** Introduction → (related work, either a short main-text section or folded into the introduction with the long version in the appendix) → setup/method → experiments → discussion with limitations → conclusion. The word "limitations" appears in the main text of 8 of 14 (a paragraph or a "Conclusion and Limitations" / "Discussion and Limitations" heading); the checklist asks for it.
- **Prior work.** Typically 40–70 references (median 54); the closest line of work is cited densely in the introduction and compared against in the experiments, with the baselines named.
- **Evidence.** The headline claim is carried by one or two main figures (error versus budget curves with intervals are the common form); per-dataset detail, further ablations and all long tables are in the appendix, which is unrestricted and is "optional reading" for reviewers (`venue.md`).

## Budget for this paper (derived from the sample and the venue's 9 pages)

- **Main text:** nine pages one column, figures and tables included. Take **6 main-text items (figures + tables) as the target and 7 as the ceiling**; the sample's median is 6 with a camera-ready page more. Each item is a full-width (5.5 in) or two half-width (≈2.7 in) panel group; at ≈0.25–0.35 page each the items take about two pages, leaving about seven for text.
- **The outline's ten items** (Fig. 1–6, Table 1–4) do not all fit: the sample suggests Fig. 1 (schematic, can be small), Table 2 + Fig. 2 (C1), Fig. 3 (C2), Fig. 5 (C4) and Fig. 4 (C3) in the main text, Fig. 6/Table 4 (C5) as one compact item or in the appendix if space is short; Tables 1 and 3 and all per-benchmark tables to the appendix. The story decides; this is the venue's budget for it.
- **Appendix:** no limit, placed after the references and before the checklist; the sample's appendices (pages after the references, estimated from file length) run roughly 12–35 pages. Material critical to a claim must not be only there (`venue.md`).
- **Abstract** target 150–230 words, one paragraph (the kit: one paragraph; the submission form: at most 5000 characters). **Introduction** target 700–1000 words with the headline result stated in it. **References** target 45–70 entries, at least the whole starter and positioning list the idea cites.
